1
00:00:03,010 --> 00:00:04,611
Just weeks after reports first

2
00:00:04,751 --> 00:00:07,693
emerged that the Houthis might attack
undersea internet cables,

3
00:00:08,113 --> 00:00:11,756
the Yemeni group appears to have started
this next phase of its offensive.

4
00:00:13,777 --> 00:00:16,339
After already disrupting commercial
shipping traffic,

5
00:00:16,679 --> 00:00:18,920
the Houthis are reportedly setting their
sights

6
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:21,802
on holding global communication systems to
ransom.

7
00:00:23,323 --> 00:00:27,386
At least four important undersea internet
cables were recently damaged,

8
00:00:27,666 --> 00:00:29,487
according to reports in Israeli media.

9
00:00:31,354 --> 00:00:32,715
These cables were damaged in

10
00:00:32,835 --> 00:00:35,517
areas between the Middle Eastern country
of Saudi Arabia

11
00:00:36,497 --> 00:00:39,760
and East African nation Djibouti,
according to news outlet Globes.

12
00:00:41,401 --> 00:00:44,943
The report said that the damage to the
cables had caused severe disruption

13
00:00:45,083 --> 00:00:47,084
of communication between Europe and Asia,

14
00:00:48,025 --> 00:00:50,747
and the biggest victims have been Gulf
countries and India.

15
00:01:07,408 --> 00:01:10,429
One of the damaged undersea systems is the
AAE-1,

16
00:01:10,909 --> 00:01:12,830
which connects East Asia to Europe.

17
00:01:13,370 --> 00:01:16,871
It links China to the West via countries
like Pakistan and Qatar.

18
00:01:17,831 --> 00:01:21,032
Then there is the Europe-India Gateway or
EIG system,

19
00:01:21,572 --> 00:01:23,633
which links Europe to Asia and Africa.

20
00:01:24,453 --> 00:01:28,554
The cable connects Southern Europe to
Egypt, Saudi Arabia, Djibouti,

21
00:01:28,874 --> 00:01:30,735
the United Arab Emirates and India.

22
00:01:31,857 --> 00:01:35,319
Another damaged cable is the CCOM, which
links European,

23
00:01:35,559 --> 00:01:38,420
African and Asian countries like South
Africa and India.

24
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:43,022
The fourth damaged cable is the TGN, which
was deployed in 2001

25
00:01:43,282 --> 00:01:45,744
by American company Tyco and sold in

26
00:01:46,004 --> 00:01:51,326
2005 for $130 million to Indian firm VSNL,
which is now Tata Communications.

27
00:01:53,747 --> 00:01:56,449
The Globe's report has said that while the
damage is severe,

28
00:01:56,769 --> 00:01:58,490
the situation is not yet critical.

29
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:02,463
This is because many other cables pass
through the same location

30
00:02:02,843 --> 00:02:04,164
connecting the three continents.

31
00:02:05,045 --> 00:02:09,748
But repairing the damaged cables might be
a difficult and dangerous task. Firstly,

32
00:02:09,789 --> 00:02:12,291
the repair work is expected to take many
weeks,

33
00:02:12,751 --> 00:02:15,693
at least 8 weeks or 2 months according to
some estimates.

34
00:02:17,855 --> 00:02:18,315
Secondly,

35
00:02:18,396 --> 00:02:22,339
communication companies might find it difficult to
hire firms to repair the cables

36
00:02:22,579 --> 00:02:24,260
because of the threat of Houthi attacks.

37
00:02:25,081 --> 00:02:28,564
This would make the work costlier as they
would charge a higher risk premium.

38
00:02:32,101 --> 00:02:34,582
Reports about the damage have emerged just
weeks

39
00:02:34,722 --> 00:02:37,162
after Yemen's internationally recognized
government

40
00:02:37,562 --> 00:02:41,363
first raised an alert about the Houthi
plan. The Yemeni government,

41
00:02:41,723 --> 00:02:43,844
which is fighting a civil war with the
Houthis,

42
00:02:44,184 --> 00:02:46,585
had cited a Telegram channel linked to the
Houthis.

43
00:02:48,205 --> 00:02:51,686
The Telegram channel had posted a map
purportedly showing the location of

44
00:02:51,806 --> 00:02:54,047
undersea internet cables running near
Yemen.

45
00:02:54,887 --> 00:02:56,887
The caption on the map had a sinister
message,

46
00:02:57,327 --> 00:02:59,568
saying that Yemen is in a strategic
location.

47
00:03:00,063 --> 00:03:02,464
with internet lines connecting not just
countries,

48
00:03:02,804 --> 00:03:04,885
but entire continents passing near it.

49
00:03:06,526 --> 00:03:09,087
Reports suggest that cables in the Red Sea
carry around

50
00:03:09,187 --> 00:03:11,488
17% of the world's internet traffic.

51
00:03:13,508 --> 00:03:15,549
When the Houthi threat first grabbed
headlines,

52
00:03:15,929 --> 00:03:18,510
experts were skeptical about whether the
Yemeni group

53
00:03:18,811 --> 00:03:22,772
would be able to carry out the attacks.
This is because these fiber optic cables

54
00:03:23,112 --> 00:03:27,094
lie hundreds of meters below the surface,
so divers cannot reach them.

55
00:03:28,643 --> 00:03:29,264
Countries like

56
00:03:29,384 --> 00:03:33,307
America and Russia have deep-sea submersibles
which are deployed from a mothership.

57
00:03:33,987 --> 00:03:37,950
The submersibles then dive to the cables
and literally cut them with tools

58
00:03:38,211 --> 00:03:39,952
which work like a giant pair of scissors.

59
00:03:40,893 --> 00:03:43,795
While it's not clear whether the Houthis
have such submersibles,

60
00:03:44,195 --> 00:03:47,178
the US military did make a chilling
announcement on February 18.

61
00:03:49,618 --> 00:03:53,720
The American military said that it had
destroyed an unmanned underwater vessel

62
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:56,701
or a drone submarine in Houthi-controlled
territory.

63
00:03:57,421 --> 00:04:00,423
The US forces said that this was the first
known use

64
00:04:00,723 --> 00:04:02,384
of a drone submarine by the Houthis

65
00:04:02,764 --> 00:04:05,905
since they began their attacks in the Red
Sea over the Gaza war.

66
00:04:07,726 --> 00:04:10,047
Houthi attacks on commercial ships in the
Red Sea have

67
00:04:10,167 --> 00:04:12,648
already caused severe disruption of
maritime trade.

68
00:04:15,538 --> 00:04:19,579
The Red Sea route accounts for around 15%
of global shipping traffic,

69
00:04:20,139 --> 00:04:22,499
and many companies have decided to avoid
the route

70
00:04:22,879 --> 00:04:28,000
and sail all the way around Africa
instead. But this has severely increased costs

71
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:30,221
and also caused delays in shipments.

72
00:04:32,041 --> 00:04:35,821
Even regular airstrikes since January by
America and some of its allies

73
00:04:36,142 --> 00:04:37,542
have failed to deter the Houthis.

74
00:04:38,422 --> 00:04:41,502
Now, if they are attacking global
communication infrastructure,

75
00:04:41,943 --> 00:04:44,423
it would mark a serious escalation of the
conflict.

76
00:04:46,102 --> 00:04:50,545
Recently, the leader of the Ansarullah or
Houthi group, Abdul Malik Al-Houthi,

77
00:04:50,845 --> 00:04:54,547
denied that he had any plans to start
attacking undersea internet cables.

78
00:04:55,607 --> 00:04:59,309
Al-Houthi called the earlier reports
confusion and distortion of his plans.

79
00:05:00,890 --> 00:05:04,272
But if any fibre optic cables near Yemen
have suffered damage,

80
00:05:04,672 --> 00:05:07,734
the needle of suspicion would immediately
swing towards the Houthis.

81
00:05:08,775 --> 00:05:12,737
This also carries the risk of drawing
other affected countries into the conflict.

82
00:05:14,486 --> 00:05:16,869
Just two days after Abdul Malik
al-Houthi's claim,

83
00:05:17,309 --> 00:05:20,834
the chief of the Indian Navy said that
Indian warships deployed near Yemen

84
00:05:21,194 --> 00:05:25,259
are tracking Houthi missiles and drones
and stand ready to respond if threatened.

